{"agent":"fleet-fixes-h","data":{"by":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"The new disjunct does not test what the docstring now claims (\"is the base tip or an earlier tip of the base\"); it returns `True` for *any* commit reachable from the base, not just a commit that was the base's tip when the merge started. `git merge-base --is-ancestor <MERGE_HEAD> <base>` is satisfied by every ancestor of the base, including commits that were never a tip of the base (e.g. the tip of a side branch that was merged into the base). Concrete scenario: `main` contains a merge of `teammate` whose tip is `T`; on `feature` the user runs `git merge teammate` so `MERGE_HEAD == T`, `T` is an ancestor of `main` but was never `main`'s tip, and the base's rules file changed after `feature` forked. With the old code `_concludes_merge_of(here, \"main\")` is `False` and the commit is refused (the remedy asked for is to merge *main*); with this line it is `True`, so `git commit` is exempted even though the merge concluded is of `teammate`, not of the base. The pre-existing guarantee exercised by `test_merging_something_other_than_the_base_is_not_exempt` only covers a side branch that is *not* an ancestor of the base, so it does not catch this widening. If the intent really is \"an earlier tip of the base\", the check needs to consult the base's reflog (or otherwise pin MERGE_HEAD to a former tip of `base`), not mere ancestry.","digest":"c71be390beab0db7","location":"ddflow/services/enforce.py:2181 (the new `merge-base --is-ancestor head.out base` branch of `_concludes_merge_of`)","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/enforce.py:2181 (the new `merge-base --is-ancestor head.out base` branch of `_concludes_merge_of`)"}],"chunks_total":1,"coverage":"1/1 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":2723,"diff_sha":"1be6edb8d8b8773cbfa1d3b8df46de92a231ee168b84d404e9e3aca8c1b9484f","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in [